  "query_preview": "..\n\nUpper level flow will turn southwesterly today, directing high\nlevel clouds over southeastern Arizona while keeping\ntemperatures 1-4 degrees above normal. This upper level wind\nshift is part of the beginning of a pattern evolution that will\nsee an eastern Pacific trough deepen and carry a sub-tropical\njet over the region through the end of the week. A poleward push\nof unseasonably moist air will arrive with this jet, bringing\n10-20 percent chances for showers and thunderstorms to eastern\nGraham, Greenlee, and Cochise counties Thursday afternoon and\nevening. The moisture plume will then overspread the region on \nFriday, with precipitable water values exceeding 1 inch in some\nareas (around the 99th percentile climatologically for this \ntime of year). With southeastern Arizona under the favorable \nleft exit region of the sub-tropical jet, chances for showers \nand thunderstorms are forecast across the area, especially from\nnorthern Pima through Pinal and Graham/Greenlee counties. In \nthese areas guidance has been consistent on the potential for \ngreater rainfall amounts as repeated rounds or training \nthunderstorms may produce a flash flood threat. Flooding \npotential will depend on how well instability can develop during\nthe day, which will also impact a low end but non-zero severe \nthunderstorm threat. With effective shear of around 20-25 kts \nand MUCAPE potentially exceeding 1000 J/kg, heavy rainfall \nrates, isolated strong gusty winds, and small hail may accompany\nany thunderstorm Friday. \n\nSaturday will mainly be between precipitation rounds as a deeper\nPacific trough makes its way towards the region. Atmospheric\nmoisture will remain on the higher side on Saturday so while\nnotable precipitation chances aren't forecast, isolated\nafternoon/evening showers can't be ruled out.\n\nA more potent mid-level shortwave and jet streak is expected to\ncross southern California and then Arizona Sunday into early \nMonday. Impressive synoptic forcing combined with a plume of \n1-1.5 inch PWATs will bring widespread rain with isolated \nembedded thunderstorms, starting in the west/north Sunday \nmorning and moving into southeastern areas through the \nday. The combination of the upper level wave and moisture plume\nwill be notable as precipitable water values will be pushing \nrecord levels for this time of year. In NAEFS climatology, \nprecipitable water and integrated water vapor transport values \nrange from the 99th percentile to the max of climatology, \nhighlighting the potential significance of this event. \nInstability may be hard to come by with widespread cloud cover \nand while this may somewhat temper short term precipitation \nrates, longer duration periods of moderate to locally heavy \nrainfall rates will still likely cause flows in normally dry \nwashes and water across roadway dips and other low-water\ncrossings. Ensemble model probabilities for rainfall range from\n0.5-1 inches across southern locations mainly south of I-10, to\n1-2 inches north through Pinal, Graham, and Greenlee counties\ninto central Arizona. These probabilities are greatest in \nhigher terrain from the Catalinas/Rincons northeast through the \nWhite Mountains. Rainfall totals pushing 3 inches can't be ruled\nout along southwest oriented slopes of this higher terrain. \nAlong with a flash flooding potential, more substantial flows \nmay develop in creeks and rivers of northern Graham and Greenlee\ncounties, including the San Francisco, Blue, and Gila rivers. \nDepending on the magnitude and placement of the heaviest \nrainfall, impacts within these watersheds could continue into \nearly next week. \n\nThe moisture feed will have likely exited the region by Monday\nmorning, but lingering moisture and cyclonic flow aloft will\nkeep a non-zero chance of a lingering shower during the day. \nTemperatures are expected to fall well below normal Sunday and\nMonday with some warming but still remaining below normal into \nmid- week.",
